var tafsirData_70 = {
  "1": "The opening of this surah draws our attention to a startling incident. A man, identified in authentic reports as al-Nadr ibn al-Harith or another Qurayshi disbeliever, arrogantly asked for the punishment of Allah to descend immediately. He said words to the effect of, “O Allah, if this is the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us.” Allah calls this person a “challenger,” someone who not only questions the warning but mocks it and demands that it be hastened. The very structure of the Arabic word points to a question that is really a scoffing dare. And Allah’s response is immediate: the punishment he demands is no illusion; it is “bound to come.” The word used here indicates something that will definitely occur, something that is inevitable and unstoppable. It is interesting that the challenger asks for the punishment as though he could control its timing, but Allah makes it clear that this coming event is not subject to human demands. It will arrive precisely when He wills, and nothing can delay it. This reminds every reader that Divine justice is not a game. Sometimes people mock warnings about the Hereafter, thinking that if it were real, it should happen now. But that boldness stems from a false sense of security. The punishment is not just a possibility; it is a predetermined reality heading toward the disbelievers. By beginning with this vivid scene, the surah immediately confronts us with human arrogance in the face of the unseen. The historical background does not limit the lesson to that one man; rather, it becomes a timeless warning for anyone who treats the Day of Judgment as a distant joke. The phrase “bound to come” plants a sense of certainty that should shake the heart of any person who reflects, because it means that the ultimate accountability is not a myth but an approaching truth.",
  "2": "This verse completes the thought of the first verse by specifying whom that certain punishment will strike: the disbelievers. It is important to note that the punishment demanded by the mocker will not fall randomly but will precisely target those who reject faith after truth has been made clear to them. The expression “to be averted by none” underscores a total absence of any defender, intercessor, or obstacle that could push that punishment aside. In this world, a powerful person might shield someone from a legal penalty, or a friend might pay a ransom to cancel a sentence. But in the matter of Divine decree, no such avenues exist. Allah alone commands, and when His punishment comes, no force in creation can repel it. This is a profound reassurance for the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, who endured constant mockery. The mockers thought that their wealth, status, or idols could protect them. Here Allah declares that on the Day the challenger was asking about, all imagined protectors will evaporate. The verse also carries a subtle refutation of the idea that the punishment might be avoided through cleverness or negotiation. The word “none” sweeps away every possibility. Even the angels, the prophets, or the most beloved servants of Allah cannot divert what He has decreed for those who stubbornly disbelieve. The verse thus reinforces the absolute sovereignty of Allah. It teaches us that while Allah’s mercy is vast, His justice is equally certain. For the disbelievers who persist in rejection, the outcome is sealed, not because Allah wishes to harm without cause, but because they have turned away from the very means of salvation. And this outcome is unchangeable. The listener is urged to reflect: if earthly consequences often catch up with wrongdoers despite their connections, how much more certain is the consequence of denying the Creator Himself?",
  "3": "The punishment mentioned in the previous verse is not some impersonal cosmic force; it comes “from Allah,” the source of all authority. He is described here with a majestic title: “Lord of pathways of heavenly ascent.” The Arabic word “Ma‘arij” means ascents, stairways, or pathways by which noble beings rise upward. This description immediately expands our vision. The One who will send the punishment is not a distant, uninvolved deity. He is the Lord of the entire celestial realm, the owner of the routes by which the angels and the souls ascend to Him. The image is that of a vast, perfectly ordered spiritual cosmos where everything ascends toward the Divine presence through designated paths. This title counters the pagan notion that Allah is remote or that the angels are independent deities. Instead, they all ascend to Him, acknowledging His supreme authority. The pathways also suggest continuous movement; the angels constantly ascend and descend, carrying out commands, recording deeds, and bringing revelation. All of these ascents end with Allah. So the punishment that will come is from the One who is high above all creation and to whom everything ultimately returns. By mentioning the ascents, the verse subtly reminds us that our own deeds and prayers rise toward Allah through these very pathways. The connection is deep: the same Lord who receives the angels’ pure worship also sees the arrogance of the challenger. The mockery does not go unnoticed; it ascends to Him, and the response descends as certain punishment. This reassures the believers that the denials they hear from the disbelievers are not hidden from the Lord of the highest realms. He is fully aware and will deal with every matter in its proper time.",
  "4": "This verse gives a breathtaking glimpse into the unseen world that operates through those heavenly pathways. The angels ascend through them continuously, but a specific and momentous ascent is highlighted: that of the “holy spirit,” which is the angel Jibril (Gabriel), and the other angels on a particular Day. That Day is described as “fifty thousand years in length.” Most commentators explain that this refers to the Day of Judgment. From the perspective of a disbeliever, that Day will feel unimaginably long due to the severity of its terrors, whereas for a believer it may pass more lightly. The number fifty thousand is not merely a figure; it conveys a sense of vastness that human minds struggle to grasp. The angels and Jibril will ascend on that Day, possibly with the records of deeds, or for the final judgment to be decreed. The mention of the holy spirit alongside the angels honours Jibril’s special rank as the carrier of revelation. This verse connects the certainty of punishment with the reality of the spiritual realm. The challenger who demanded that punishment might have mocked the notion of angels and ascents. So Allah presents the image of that very Day when the angels, whom they ridiculed, will ascend to Him, and the matter will be settled. The length of fifty thousand years also demonstrates that Allah’s reckoning of time is utterly different from ours. A period that seems impossible to human patience is nothing before the Divine plan. This teaches the believers to place their trust in Allah’s timing rather than being hasty. The ascent of the angels further implies that their witness against the disbelievers will be complete. Every deed, including the mocking question, will have been recorded by those noble beings who now ascend to present it. The verse therefore intertwines warning with a majestic reminder of the unseen order that the disbelievers deny but which is absolutely real.",
  "5": "After revealing the certainty of the punishment and the vast reality of the heavenly ascents, Allah turns directly to the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, with a gentle but firm command: “So endure... with beautiful patience.” The Arabic term “sabr jameel” is a special kind of patience. It is a patience that contains no complaining, no anxiety that shows outwardly, and no haste for the fulfilment of the threat. The Prophet had to face daily mockery, insults, and pressure from the very people whom he was trying to save. One of them had just publicly challenged Allah to send the punishment. In such a situation, a human being might feel anger or despair. But Allah instructs him to respond with beautiful patience. This is patience that is full of dignity, calmness, and complete trust that Allah will handle the matter. It is not passive weakness but active restraint and steadfastness. The word “beautiful” tells us that patience itself can become a form of da‘wah. When the Prophet remained composed despite provocation, it testified to the truth of his message. This command is also a timeless lesson for every believer. When we see wrongdoing and mockery of faith, the immediate impulse might be to retaliate harshly. But the Quran teaches that the ultimate response lies with Allah. The role of the believer is not to force the punishment but to continue conveying the truth and to leave the outcome to the Lord of the ascents. By linking this command to the earlier verses, Allah provides the foundation for that patience: the Prophet can be patient because he knows that the punishment is certain and that the angels’ ascent witnesses everything. He is not ignoring injustice; he is entrusting it to the One who will settle it on a Day fifty thousand years long. Beautiful patience, then, is the strength that comes from deep conviction.",
  "6": "The surah now explains why the disbelievers act as they do and why the Prophet needs such patience. They “truly see this Day as impossible.” The word “truly” adds emphasis, showing that their conviction is sincere in their own minds. From their materialistic perspective, the idea of a resurrection, a judgment that lasts fifty thousand years, and a punishment coming from the pathways of ascent seems completely far-fetched. They measure everything by their immediate experience. They see bodies decay into dust, and they cannot conceive how a new creation could occur. Their own arrogance prevents them from accepting that a power beyond their comprehension could bring it about. This verse captures a fundamental psychological barrier to faith: when people limit reality to what they can observe, the unseen becomes “impossible” by definition. The verse does not say they merely doubt; it says they see it as impossible, indicating a settled conviction. This mindset leads them to mock and challenge. They think that by demanding the punishment immediately they are exposing the Prophet’s warning as empty. They do not realize that their perception is flawed. Allah is exposing their inner state not to excuse them but to show how far their reasoning has strayed. And for the Prophet, knowing this helps him understand that their mockery is not a result of a lack of evidence but of a deeper rejection rooted in their worldview. The verse thus sets up a stark contrast with the next verse. It is a reminder that human judgment is often based on a very narrow window of experience, while the full reality is known only to the Creator. Recognizing this, a believer should not be shaken when the majority dismiss the Day of Judgment as impossible.",
  "7": "In direct contrast to the disbelievers’ perception, Allah declares, “but We see it as inevitable.” The Arabic wording uses a form of intense certainty; Allah does not merely predict the Day, He beholds it as an established fact. The difference between human seeing and Divine seeing is infinite. Humans look at scattered bones and see impossibility. Allah looks at the same and sees the approaching reality of resurrection. When He says “We see,” it reflects His perfect knowledge that transcends past, present, and future. The Day is inevitable not because it is determined by some blind fate, but because Allah has willed it, planned it, and in His sight it is already a present truth. This verse offers the greatest comfort to the Prophet and to all believers. The disbelievers may “truly” consider it impossible, but their truth is only a subjective impression, whereas Allah’s “seeing” is objective reality. The message is that the mockery does not alter the outcome in the slightest. The challenger demanded a punishment bound to come, and here Allah affirms that the Day containing that punishment is inevitable. The patient endurance commanded in verse five is sustained by this assurance. If a believer is walking a path and others say the destination does not exist, but the mapmaker who created the entire landscape confirms that it is just ahead, the traveller continues with confidence. That is the dynamic here. The verse also subtly warns the mockers: their insistence that the Day is impossible does not protect them. They are like someone who denies the sunrise while standing in the dark before dawn. Allah’s vision encompasses the whole timeline, and He tells us that this Day is unquestionably coming.",
  "8": "Having established the inevitability of the Day, the surah now draws a vivid picture of its horrors. The first image is cosmic: “On that Day the sky will be like molten brass.” The sky, which we know as a vast, stable expanse of blue or starry black, will undergo a terrifying transformation. It will become like molten brass—something that once seemed solid is now a turbulent, boiling liquid, or like the dregs of oil, as some commentators explain. The comparison conveys extreme heat, heavy turbidity, and a complete alteration of the natural order. The sky is often mentioned in the Quran as a symbol of order and protection, a canopy without visible pillars. On that Day, that canopy will become a source of threat. The molten brass imagery may also suggest a dark, reddish, oppressive hue, like metal that has been heated to an unbearable degree and is seething. This transformation is not a random change; it is the prelude to the end of the universe as we know it and the beginning of a new creation. The verse forces the imagination to confront the complete collapse of everything that feels secure. For the disbelievers who saw the Day as impossible, this description challenges their comfortable assumptions. The very heavens they consider permanent will dissolve. The molten brass is also a visual representation of the intensity of the punishment that the challenger had mockingly demanded. If the sky itself is in such a state, what will be the condition of those who are awaiting judgment? The believer who hears this should feel a profound sense of awe and a renewed resolve to live in a way that prepares for that Day, while the skeptic is invited to reflect on the power that can reshape the cosmos so utterly.",
  "9": "Alongside the sky’s horrifying change, the surah describes another mighty feature of the earth: “and the mountains like tufts of wool.” Mountains are the ultimate symbols of stability, weight, and permanence. They anchor the earth and have stood for ages. On the Day of Judgment, however, they will be reduced to something as light and insubstantial as tufts of carded wool. The image is that of coloured wool being fluffed up and blown about by the wind. What was once solid and unshakable becomes weightless and scattered. The Quran uses similar imagery elsewhere, describing mountains as being like carded wool or as being set into motion. This degradation is part of the complete leveling of the earth before the resurrection. The verse contrasts the immense power of Allah with the fragility of what humans consider enduring. The disbelievers who trusted in their strongholds, their high mountains, and their physical world will see it all turn to nothingness. The mention of wool also evokes a sense of lightness that can be carried away effortlessly. This imagery, paired with the molten sky, creates a scene of total dissolution of the familiar world. It is a Day when all the solidity of dunya is unmasked as temporary. For the believer, this is a humbling reminder not to attach one’s heart to the seeming permanence of this world. The mountains that we admire for their grandeur will one day be like flimsy fibres. If that is the end of the mightiest creation, where does the arrogant human stand? This verse thus deepens the warning and prepares the listener for the interpersonal horrors that follow. The external world collapses, and then the internal world of human relationships also shatters.",
  "10": "Amid the cosmic chaos, human ties will disintegrate. On that Day, “no close friend will ask about their friends.” The Arabic word for “close friend” is “hameem,” which denotes an intimate, warm, affectionate companion—someone whose closeness is like that of family. In this world, a true friend would naturally inquire about the well-being of another, especially in times of distress. But the terror of that Day will be so overwhelming that even this instinct will be suspended. Every soul will be entirely absorbed with its own situation, gasping for its own salvation. The verse does not say they will not care; it says they will not even ask. The panic will erase normal social courtesies and even the deepest bonds of love. This paints a picture of utter isolation in a crowd. People will be together, but each will be locked in solitary dread. This psychological torment is part of the punishment. The disbeliever who lived relying on his friendships, his social circle, and his tribal alliances will find them all useless. The verse also silently answers the mocker who may have thought that his people could protect him. All those connections will be severed by the horror of the scene. The implication for the believer is profound: the only relationship that will truly benefit on that Day is one’s relationship with Allah. All other friendships, however sincere, must be rooted in faith to survive the shock. The verse thus sets the stage for the next, where people will actually see each other but still ignore those dearest to them, and then even attempt to ransom themselves at the expense of their loved ones.",
  "11": "The scene intensifies with a painful paradox: “although they will be made to see each other.” Allah will cause them to see one another, meaning that the separation is not due to physical distance or darkness. They will be in plain view, yet the psychological gulf will be so vast that the normal bonds of concern will vanish. This seeing makes the agony sharper. A father will see his son, a friend will see his companion, but instead of moving to help, each will be paralyzed by his own terror. Then the verse zooms in on “the wicked,” those who earned the punishment through their disbelief and evil deeds. In that state, they will desperately wish to ransom themselves. The first and most shocking offer: they would sacrifice their own children. A parent’s love for a child is normally the strongest natural instinct. To wish to give up one’s child in exchange for personal safety shows that the punishment they face has shattered every natural impulse. The Arabic word for ransom implies a transaction: they would pay with their children if that were accepted. But it will not be. The very thought exposes the horror of the situation; a person would contemplate the unthinkable because the torment is so intense. This and the following verses peel away the layers of relationships that a person holds dear—children, then spouse, then siblings, then clan, and finally all of humanity. It is a descending list from the closest bond outward, showing that even the most cherished connections will be offered up in a futile bid to escape. This stark warning is meant to shake the living into reevaluating what they truly rely on.",
  "12": "The list of desperate ransom attempts continues. After children, the wicked person would offer his or her “spouses” and “siblings.” A spouse is one’s life partner, the person with whom one shares the most intimate moments, secrets, and daily life. The bond of marriage is intended by Allah to be one of love and mercy. Yet on that Day, the same spouse will be seen not as a source of comfort but as a bargaining chip to avoid the Fire. The disbeliever would say, in effect, “Take my wife, my husband, and let me go free.” This reveals the complete erosion of loyalty and affection under the weight of Divine justice. Likewise, siblings—brothers and sisters who grew up together, shared childhood, and defended each other against outsiders—will be offered up with the same desperate hope. In a tribal society like that of Quraysh, sibling loyalty was legendary. This verse tells them that such bonds are worthless on the Day of Reckoning without faith. The imagery shatters any illusion that family ties alone can intercede or protect. The sequence is important: first the child, then the spouse, then the sibling. It moves from offspring to partner to blood relation, showing that every human category of closeness will be severed. For the believers, this is not a message to love their families less, but to ensure that love for Allah and His messenger comes first, so that those relationships can be eternal in Paradise rather than severed in Hell. It reminds us that the only unbreakable bond is the bond of iman. All else can be shattered by selfish terror on that Day.",
  "13": "The circle widens further to include “their clan that sheltered them.” In pre-Islamic Arab society and in many cultures, the clan or extended tribe was the ultimate safety net. If a person was in trouble, his clan would mobilize to protect him, no matter the cost. A man’s honour was tied to the strength of his clan. The Arabic word used here paints a picture of a group that gives refuge, that surrounds a person like a shield. The wicked disbeliever, in his desperation, would now throw that entire protective structure into the flames to save his own skin. He would offer his whole tribe—the very people who gave him identity, status, and security—as a ransom. The irony is bitter: the clan that once sheltered him from worldly harm cannot shelter him from Allah’s punishment, and he in turn would abandon them instantly. This verse delivers a powerful blow to the tribal pride of the Quraysh. They thought their collective strength made them invincible. Allah informs them that on that Day, each individual will be so consumed by personal terror that the clan will become a currency to be spent in vain. The verse also teaches a universal lesson about misplaced reliance. People often think that belonging to a strong group—be it a nation, a political party, or a social class—will protect them in ultimate matters. But here, even the tightest human collective is useless. Salvation is individual, depending on one’s faith and deeds. The imagery of the sheltering clan being traded away highlights the absolute loneliness of the moment of judgment.",
  "14": "Reaching the farthest extent of this desperate bargaining, the wicked person would offer not just his children, spouse, siblings, or clan, but “everyone on earth altogether.” This is the ultimate sum. The person would sacrifice all of humanity—every man, woman, and child—if only he could be spared from the punishment. The phrase “just to save themselves” underscores the singular, selfish goal. The plural “themselves” in Arabic can be understood as each individual acting for his own self. It is the final statement of how the terror of that Day isolates a person and extinguishes all altruism and social feeling. The disbeliever who in this world might have boasted about his generosity or his care for people would, in that moment, trade the entire human race for his own safety. This is not an exaggeration; it is a divinely revealed portrait of the psychological reality of Hell’s approach. It demonstrates that the torment is of a magnitude that renders all worldly values meaningless. The verse serves as the climax of the ransom sequence. After this, the door of ransom is slammed shut with the next verse. Allah is showing that even if a person possessed all the world’s inhabitants to offer, it would not be accepted. The only way to escape that punishment is to avoid deserving it in the first place through faith and righteousness. The verse confronts every listener with the profound question: what am I holding onto today that I think can save me, and will it be of any use there?",
  "15": "After the vivid depiction of futile ransom offers, the response comes with a decisive and forceful rejection: “But no!” The Arabic word “kalla” is a strong particle of negation that slams the door on all those desperate hopes. It means absolutely not—none of those ransoms will be accepted, no escape is possible. Immediately following this negation, Allah introduces the reality that awaits: “There will certainly be a raging Flame.” The word for “raging Flame” here is “laza,” one of the names of Hellfire. It is a fire that blazes with intense, leaping flames, a fire that is not still but actively, fiercely burning. The description as “raging” captures the furious, untamable nature of this punishment. It is not a passive fire; it is almost alive in its fury, stoked by Divine command. The certainty is emphasized by the emphatic particle; this Flame is real and unavoidable for the wicked. After the psychological torment of severed relationships, now the physical torment is introduced. The earlier verses described the sky and mountains, but now the focus narrows directly onto what the disbeliever will face. This transition from cosmic horror to personal punishment makes the warning intensely direct. The phrase also echoes the first verse: the challenger demanded a punishment, and here it is named—Laza, the raging Flame. Allah answers mockery with a terrifying reality. For believers, this verse deepens their gratitude for the faith that saves them from such an end, and it rekindles a healthy fear that keeps them on the straight path. The imagery of the flame that will not be quenched sets the stage for the graphic descriptions that follow.",
  "16": "The description of Laza’s effect is shockingly precise: “ripping off scalps.” The Arabic verb “nazza‘ah” indicates an aggressive pulling or tearing away. The Flame does not merely burn the skin; it violently tears the scalp right off the head. The scalp is the covering of the skull, the place where hair grows. To have it ripped away is an excruciating form of torture. This image tells us that the Fire of Hell is not a uniform, gentle burn. It attacks the extremities, the very parts of the body that are most sensitive. The scalp contains countless nerve endings, and its removal would cause unimaginable pain. Moreover, the mention of the scalp specifically may carry a symbolic meaning. In pre-Islamic Arab culture, the head was a symbol of honour and pride. People took great pride in their hair and head coverings. To have the scalp ripped off is a sign of complete humiliation in addition to physical agony. The punishment fits the crime of arrogance. Those who walked on earth with heads held high in rebellion against Allah will find those same heads subjected to the worst torment. The verse also demonstrates the comprehensive nature of the punishment; every part of the body will experience its share of suffering. This single, vivid detail is enough to make any person shudder. It serves as a powerful deterrent, encouraging the believer to protect his or her faith, and warning the skeptic that the mockery of the Fire is no light matter. The intensity of the imagery is meant to penetrate hearts that have become hardened.",
  "17": "The raging Flame is not a random force; it has a specific target. The verse says, “It will summon...” The Fire is personified as if it can call out by name. It will recognize and pull toward itself those who deserve it. The ones summoned are described with two characteristics. First, “whoever turned their backs on Allah,” meaning those who rejected faith, who actively turned away from the worship and obedience of their Creator. This turning of the back is a physical metaphor for abandonment and denial. Second, “and turned away from the truth.” This further clarifies that their rejection was not passive ignorance; it was an active aversion after the truth had been presented to them. They saw the signs, they heard the message, but they deliberately distanced themselves. The combination of “turned their backs” and “turned away” emphasizes the completeness of their refusal. They did not merely disbelieve; they opposed and fled from the truth. The Fire summoning them adds a chilling sense of destiny. In this world, they turned away from the call of the prophets. In the Hereafter, the Fire will call them, and they will have no choice but to respond. This is a precise reversal. The word “summon” also evokes the image of a caller inviting people, much like the caller to faith. Those who refused the invitation to guidance will be forced to accept the invitation to punishment. The verse ties the horrific physical descriptions to the moral causes, leaving no doubt about the justice of the punishment. It is a direct answer to the challenger: the punishment you mockingly asked for will not miss you; it knows its own people.",
  "18": "The second category of those summoned by the Fire is those who “gathered and hoarded wealth.” This describes a person whose primary concern in life was the accumulation of riches, not for a legitimate purpose, but to store and withhold it. The Arabic words suggest gathering wealth and then locking it away, keeping it from circulation, refusing to give the poor their due, and loving the piles of coins for their own sake. The disbelievers of Quraysh were often wealthy merchants who prided themselves on their fortunes. This verse exposes their sin. It is not merely having wealth that is condemned, but the obsessive gathering and hoarding of it without fulfilling the rights of Allah. By pairing this with the previous verse, we see that turning away from Allah is often accompanied by a love of materialism that hardens the heart. The fire will summon such people because their wealth, which they thought would secure their future, will testify against them. They gathered it and refused to use it in the path of the truth, so the Fire gathers them. The justice is perfect. This verse also warns the believers against the disease of hoarding. Even if one prays, if the heart becomes enslaved to wealth and refuses to give zakat and charity, the danger is real. The surah will later describe the qualities of the faithful, contrasting their open-handed generosity with this destructive hoarding. The imagery of a Fire that calls out to the stingy and the arrogant ties together the themes of this passage: the punishment is not arbitrary; it is the direct result of one’s own choices.",
  "19": "With this verse, the surah shifts from the horrific scenes of the Day to a profound analysis of human nature. “Indeed, humankind was created impatient.” The word used here is “halu‘a,” which denotes a state of intense anxiety, restlessness, and impatience. It describes someone who is easily disturbed, who cannot bear discomfort calmly, and who is excessively eager for good and panicked by evil. Allah is telling us that this trait is part of the original makeup of the human being. It is not an acquired habit but an inborn characteristic. We are created with this tendency because it serves a purpose: it makes us constantly active, seeking what benefits us and avoiding what harms us. However, left unchecked, it becomes a destructive spiritual flaw. The verse explains why the disbelievers behave as they do. Their mockery, their demand for immediate punishment, their clinging to wealth, and their rejection of the Hereafter all stem from this deep-seated impatience. They want good right now, they cannot tolerate waiting for unseen promises, and they cannot handle hardship with trust in Allah. This diagnosis is crucial because it shows that the cure for unbelief lies not merely in logical arguments but in training the soul to overcome its raw, hasty nature. The verse is not a statement of despair; rather, it lays the groundwork for the exception that follows: “except those who pray.” It tells us that while this impatient nature is the default, it can be transformed through faith and worship. So the believer understands that the struggle against impatience is a core part of spiritual growth. This verse links beautifully back to the command of beautiful patience in verse five. The Prophet is to endure with patience, and now we learn why patience is so essential: because our very creation tends toward impatience.",
  "20": "This verse unpacks the first manifestation of that impatient nature: “distressed when touched with evil.” When any harm, difficulty, or loss touches such a person, he falls into severe agitation and despair. The Arabic word “jazu‘a” indicates intense, uncontrolled anxiety and complaint. He cannot bear hardship. He panics, questions why this is happening, and may even turn away from faith. The petty trials of this world trigger an extreme reaction because his soul is anchored only in immediate comfort. This description fits the disbelievers who, when faced with the slightest trial, would grumble and doubt the wisdom of Allah. It also explains why they could not accept the concept of a delayed punishment; their impatient nature demands instant outcomes. For the believer, this verse serves as a mirror. When touched with evil—be it sickness, financial loss, or emotional pain—does one become distressed and overwhelmed, or does one seek patience in prayer? The description is a warning that such a reaction is a sign of the raw, untrained human condition. If left uncured by faith, it leads to the very rejection and hoarding described in earlier verses. The verse uses the word “evil” broadly, encompassing any trial, big or small. The problem is not feeling pain—that is human—but becoming psychologically shattered and losing trust in Allah. This distress is the opposite of the beautiful patience commanded in verse five. The surah thus presents a complete picture: human beings are naturally prone to this impatience, but the path of salvation lies in the qualities that will be listed immediately afterward, starting with consistent prayer. This creates a powerful transition into the description of the faithful, whose lives are governed not by hasty emotions but by deep, disciplined devotion.",
  "21": "The previous verse described the human tendency to become intensely distressed when touched by hardship. Now the surah reveals the other side of the same impatient nature: “and withholding when touched with good.” When Allah bestows blessings—wealth, health, status, or comfort—this raw human disposition clings to those gifts and refuses to share them. The Arabic word “manu‘a” means to hold back greedily, to prevent others from accessing what one possesses, and to become stingy. It is the instinct to gather and hoard, as mentioned earlier in the context of those summoned by the Fire. This withholding is not merely a financial issue; it can also manifest in withholding kindness, knowledge, or even simple gratitude. The person touched with good often forgets that the good came from Allah and imagines that it is his own permanent possession. So he tightens his grip, fearing that any sharing will diminish him. This is a direct symptom of the “halu‘” mentioned in verse nineteen—an agitated, anxious condition that panics at loss and hoards in gain. The contrast is sharp: when evil touches him, he falls apart; when good touches him, he becomes miserly. Both reactions are rooted in a lack of trust in Allah and a failure to see beyond the immediate moment. The verse exposes a profound psychological truth: the same heart that cannot patiently bear trial also cannot generously handle prosperity. Without spiritual training, both adversity and ease become traps. Adversity leads to despair, and ease leads to arrogance and selfishness. The surah is preparing to introduce the transformative power of faith and worship, which will be described in the following verses. The faithful are those who break free from this cycle. They are patient in hardship and generous in ease. This verse, then, serves as a mirror. It asks the listener: when good touches you, do you open your hands or clench your fists? It also sets the stage for the beautiful exception that begins with “except those who pray,” showing that prayer and the qualities that follow can cure this deep-seated miserliness and make a person a channel of Allah’s blessings rather than a dead end.",
  "22": "After diagnosing the human condition with its restless impatience and its flip-flopping between despair and greed, Allah opens the door of hope with a single, powerful exception: “except those who pray.” The prayer here, “salah,” is not just the physical ritual. It is the act of turning to Allah, standing before Him, and realigning the soul. The ones who pray are taken out of the default, destructive pattern because they are connected to the source of all stability. The impatient nature described earlier is a condition of the soul left to its own devices, buffeted by events. But when a human being regularly stands in prayer, he or she anchors the heart in the recognition that there is a Lord who gives and withholds, who heals and tests. This relationship completely transforms the reaction to good and evil. When evil touches a praying person, he turns to prayer for patience, rather than disintegrating into panic. When good touches him, he remembers that it is from Allah and that it carries a responsibility, so he gives rather than withholds. The verse singles out prayer as the first and most central quality of the faithful because it is the foundation upon which all other virtues are built. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said that the first deed for which a person will be held accountable on the Day of Judgment is the prayer. If it is sound, the rest of the deeds will be sound. This exception reminds us that the human default can be overcome. The “halu‘” is not an unchangeable fate; it is like a wild horse that can be tamed through the discipline of worship. Moreover, the placement here is subtle. The surah began with the deniers who mocked the Day of Judgment, then described the horrors of that Day, and then diagnosed their spiritual sickness. Now it offers the medicine: prayer. The disbelievers refused to bow to Allah, so they remained trapped in their anxious hoarding and mockery. The believers, by praying, step out of that prison. The prayer is what shifts a person from being a mere creature of impulses to being a servant of the Most High.",
  "23": "This verse adds a crucial modifier to the quality of prayer: those who are “consistently performing their prayers.” The Arabic word “da’imoon” means perpetual, constant, unceasing. It is not enough to pray once in a while, or during a crisis, or only when motivation strikes. The faithful are those who have made their prayers a permanent, uninterrupted feature of their lives. They do not abandon the prayer when they are busy, tired, or prosperous. The prayer becomes like a steady stream of water that never dries up. The Prophet, peace be upon him, was asked which deeds are most beloved to Allah, and he replied, “The most constant of them, even if they are few.” This emphasis on consistency teaches us that the transformative power of prayer is not in an occasional burst of intense devotion but in the regular, disciplined rhythm that reshapes the soul over time. The prayer times are spread throughout the day—dawn, noon, afternoon, sunset, night—ensuring that the believer repeatedly returns to Allah. Each return reinforces the cure for impatience and greed. When a person consistently prays, the habit of turning to Allah becomes stronger than the habit of panicking at hardship or clinging to wealth. The verse also implicitly refutes those who claim to believe but neglect their prayers due to laziness or worldly distractions. Such negligence is a sign that the root disease of “halu‘” has not been cured. By mentioning consistency right after naming prayer, Allah is telling us that the prayer’s benefit depends on its permanence. A broken connection is almost as ineffective as no connection. For the companions and the early Muslims, this verse was a reminder to guard the five daily prayers with their hearts and not just with their limbs. The constant prayer becomes a fortress; every time the world shakes the believer, the next prayer is only a few hours away, offering a chance to regain composure, to shed the accumulated impatience, and to realign with the Lord of the pathways of ascent.",
  "24": "Moving from the relationship with Allah to the relationship with wealth, the faithful are described as those “who give the rightful share of their wealth.” The Arabic term “haqqun ma‘lum” means a known, determined right. This refers primarily to the obligatory zakat, but it also encompasses any right that the Shariah has fixed upon wealth, such as charity to relatives, expiations, and even voluntary giving that a person has made a regular habit. The phrase teaches a profound shift in mindset: the poor and the needy have a “right” in the wealth of the well-off. It is not merely a favour or a gift from the rich to the poor; it is a debt that the wealthy owe to the vulnerable by the command of Allah. This understanding directly cures the disease of withholding described in verse twenty-one. A person who is naturally miserly and hoards when good touches him can only break free by recognizing that his wealth is not entirely his own. A portion of it belongs to others, and he is merely a trustee. By giving this rightful share, he detaches his heart from the love of hoarding and purifies his soul. The placement of this quality right after prayer is significant. The Prophet, peace be upon him, often paired prayer and zakat together in the Quran. Prayer connects the servant to his Lord, and zakat connects the servant to his fellow human beings. Both are essential for a complete faith. The one who prays but withholds the rightful share has not yet cured the “halu‘” disease, because he still clings to good with a closed hand. The early Muslims in Mecca, where this surah was revealed, were often persecuted and poor. Yet even in that hardship, the principle of giving was instilled. Those who had little were encouraged to share. The verse challenges the believer to look at his wealth and ask: have I given the known right that Allah has placed upon me? The “rightful share” is a known amount, not a vague feeling of generosity, meaning that faith requires concrete, measurable action that overcomes the selfish whims of the impatient soul.",
  "25": "The verse now specifies the recipients of that rightful share: “to the beggar and the poor.” The Arabic distinguishes between two categories. The “sa’il” is the one who asks, who stretches out his hand and makes his need known. The “mahrum” is the poor person who does not beg, perhaps out of dignity or shyness, yet remains deprived. By mentioning both, the verse teaches the believer to give both to those who openly request help and to those who suffer in silence. The sa’il is easy to identify, but the mahrum requires awareness and effort. The faithful community is expected to actively seek out those hidden poor rather than waiting for them to beg. This care for the mahrum reflects a deep sensitivity; it shows that the cure for hoarding goes beyond merely responding to requests. It involves a proactive concern for the welfare of others. In a society like pre-Islamic Mecca, the poor and the deprived were often neglected and looked down upon. The tribal chiefs and wealthy merchants hoarded their riches and paid no attention to the weak. This surah overturns that value system completely. The true believers are those who have established a recognized right in their wealth for these two groups. The pairing with the previous verse shows that giving is not optional charity; it is a binding obligation and a right. It also connects to the earlier image of the wicked person who would ransom himself with his entire clan on the Day of Judgment. That person hoarded wealth in the world and refused to give the beggar and the poor their due. Now, in the Hellfire, he offers everything to escape, but it is too late. The faithful, by giving the rightful share in this life, have already released themselves from that destructive love of wealth. They have invested in their own salvation. This instruction, revealed in Mecca, laid the foundation for the later detailed system of zakat in Medina, showing that the principle of caring for the poor was integral to Islam from the very beginning.",
  "26": "The next quality of the faithful is that they “firmly believe in the Day of Judgment.” The Arabic construction “yusaddiqoona bi yawm id-deen” implies not just a vague acceptance but an active, heart-felt confirmation. The word “deen” here means recompense—the Day when every soul will be repaid for what it earned. This belief is the anchor that keeps all the other qualities in place. Why does a person pray constantly? Because he believes he will stand before his Lord on that Day. Why does a person give the rightful share of his wealth? Because he knows that his wealth will be accounted for. Why does a person restrain his impatience and fear? Because he is certain that a just judgment is coming. The earlier part of the surah described the horrors of that Day in vivid detail: the molten sky, the mountains like wool, the severed relationships, and the raging Flame. The faithful do not see these as distant myths; they hold them as absolutely true, and this conviction shapes their actions. The placement of this quality here also contrasts with the disbelievers who saw the Day as impossible. The faithful are the opposite: they “firmly believe.” This belief is not a blind leap; it is based on the revelation of Allah and the signs within creation and within the soul. It becomes a lens through which every event is interpreted. When touched with evil, the believer remembers the Day of Judgment and says, “This trial is temporary; the ultimate justice is with Allah.” When touched with good, he remembers the Day and says, “This blessing is a test; I will be asked about it.” Thus, firm belief in the Day of Recompense directly cures the two symptoms of impatience: distress at evil and withholding at good. The surah is building a complete portrait of the transformed human being. Prayer, charity, and belief in the Hereafter form a triangle of faith that stabilizes the restless soul. Without this belief, all other efforts risk collapsing under the weight of worldly pressures.",
  "27": "The description of the faithful deepens with an inner quality: “and those who fear the punishment of their Lord.” This fear, “khawf,” is not a paralyzing terror that makes one despair of mercy; it is a reverential awe and a constant awareness that Allah’s punishment is a real possibility if one falls into disobedience. The faithful do not feel secure from the consequences of their sins. They live with a balanced consciousness that combines hope in Allah’s mercy and fear of His justice. The verse specifically links the fear to “the punishment of their Lord,” not to an abstract concept. It is personal: their Lord, who is also the Most Compassionate, can punish. This fear protects a person from becoming complacent. A believer might pray, give charity, and profess belief, but still trembles inwardly at the thought of the Day of Judgment, knowing that no deed is perfect and that only Allah’s mercy can ultimately grant entry into Paradise. This quality stands in sharp contrast to the disbelievers who mocked the warning and arrogantly demanded the punishment. They had no fear whatsoever. The faithful, on the other hand, have a sensitive heart that shudders at the mention of Hellfire. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said that if you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much. That is the fruit of this fear. It is also the antidote to the impatience described earlier. The “halu‘” person panics only at worldly loss. The believer fears something far greater—the displeasure of the Lord and the punishment of the Hereafter—so worldly hardships become relatively small in comparison. This fear fuels the beautiful patience commanded in verse five. It helps the believer endure trials because he is more afraid of failing the test than of the test itself. The verse also subtly prepares for the next verse’s explanation that no one should feel secure from this punishment.",
  "28": "This verse explains the reason behind the fear mentioned in the previous one: “knowing that none should feel secure from their Lord’s punishment.” The faithful are fully aware of a fundamental spiritual principle: absolute security from Allah’s plan is a sign of heedlessness. Even the most righteous person cannot say with certainty, “I am safe from the punishment,” because the heart can change, the end of life is unknown, and true judgment belongs only to Allah. This knowledge prevents spiritual arrogance. It is the opposite of the attitude of the disbelievers who saw the Day as impossible and felt no fear. The verse teaches that fear of Allah’s punishment is not a sign of weak faith; rather, it is a sign of deep understanding. The one who knows Allah’s power and His rights over His servants can never feel completely immune. This is not a call to despair; rather, it is a call to constant vigilance. The Prophet, peace be upon him, taught that no one enters Paradise by his deeds alone, but only by the mercy of Allah. When the companions asked, “Even you, O Messenger of Allah?” he replied, “Even me, unless Allah envelops me in His mercy.” This demonstrates the perfect balance: the greatest of creation did not feel secure, yet he was the most hopeful in Allah’s mercy. The faithful, then, live between fear and hope. The fear keeps them from sin, and the hope keeps them from giving up. In the context of the surah, this knowledge is the inner engine that drives the beautiful patience and all the other qualities. If a person felt secure from punishment, he might slack in prayer or withhold the rightful share. But knowing that none is safe, he strives continually. This verse also ties back to the challenger in the opening. That man felt completely secure, mocking the punishment. The faithful are nothing like him. They take the warning seriously, and that seriousness saves them from the very punishment they fear.",
  "29": "The surah now turns to another essential dimension of the faithful character: “and those who guard their chastity.” The Arabic phrase “hafizoona li-furujihim” literally means they are guardians over their private parts. This is a comprehensive expression for sexual purity and modesty. It means they protect themselves from all unlawful sexual relations, from immodest gazing, from lewd speech, and from any act that violates the boundaries set by Allah. Chastity is a hallmark of the true believer, and it directly counteracts the impatient, desire-driven nature described earlier. The “halu‘” person is someone who is a slave to immediate gratification. Whether it is the gratification of complaining when touched by evil or hoarding when touched by good, the root problem is the inability to delay or control impulses. Sexual desire is one of the most powerful human impulses. The faithful are those who do not let this impulse rule them; they channel it within the limits set by Allah. Guarding chastity requires patience, self-discipline, and a deep sense of accountability. This quality connects to the fear of punishment and the belief in the Day of Judgment. One who truly believes he will stand before Allah and who fears His punishment will not risk eternal loss for a fleeting pleasure. In the Meccan context, immorality and indecency were rampant. The surah is setting a new standard of purity for the emerging Muslim community. It is also worth noting that guarding chastity applies equally to men and women; the Arabic term is gender-inclusive in this context, referring to all believers. This verse leads naturally into the next two verses, which define the lawful outlet for sexual desire. By stating “those who guard their chastity” first, Allah praises the principle of restraint before mentioning the permissible exceptions. This order teaches that the default attitude of a believer is one of modesty and control, not one of seeking to fulfil desire by any means.",
  "30": "After establishing the high standard of guarding chastity, the verse immediately clarifies the lawful exceptions: “except with their wives or those bondwomen in their possession, for then they are free from blame.” The faithful are not expected to suppress the natural desire entirely; rather, Allah has provided pure and honourable outlets. The first and primary outlet is marriage to one’s wives. The marital relationship is described in the Quran as a sign of Allah’s mercy, characterized by love, tranquillity, and mutual rights. Within this bond, intimacy is not only permissible but an act that can bring reward when done with the right intention. The second outlet, “those bondwomen in their possession,” refers to a specific historical legal category: female captives of war who were rightfully owned under the rules of Islamic warfare as it existed at that time. This was a regulated institution, not a licence for exploitation. The Quran and Sunnah set strict rules for the treatment of such women, including the fact that any children born to them were free and that their masters were responsible for their well-being. It is important to understand that this ruling was part of the social reality of the time, in a world where slavery was a universal institution. Islam did not initiate it; rather, it regulated it heavily and encouraged emancipation as a highly virtuous act. The permission here is coupled with the statement “for then they are free from blame,” meaning that within these two boundaries, fulfilling one’s desires is not sinful; rather, it is something natural and clean. The verse thus strikes a perfect balance between asceticism and licentiousness. It does not demand celibacy, which can be unnatural, nor does it allow promiscuity. The faithful channel their desires through these lawful means and thereby guard their chastity outside of them. This is a profound wisdom that protects society, lineage, and individual honour.",
  "31": "The chapter closes the discussion on chastity with a firm boundary: “but whoever seeks beyond that are the transgressors.” The phrase “beyond that” means any sexual activity outside the two lawful categories of marriage and the bondwomen. Whoever crosses these limits has stepped into the realm of transgression. The Arabic word “al-‘adoon” means those who exceed the bounds, who are aggressive in their violation of Allah’s laws. This includes adultery, fornication, homosexuality, and any other form of illicit relationship. The verse is absolutely clear that there is no third permissible category. In a time when modern ideas may try to blur these lines, the Quranic ruling stands firm. The transgression here is not a light matter; it is an act of injustice against one’s own soul, against the other person involved, and against the broader social order. The surah earlier described the Fire that summons those who turned away from truth and hoarded wealth. Sexual transgression is often linked in the Quran with these very spiritual diseases. It springs from the same impatient, gratification-seeking nature that cannot wait for what is lawful and wholesome. By calling them “transgressors,” Allah indicates that such actions are not merely mistakes; they are acts of rebellion against the limits set by the All-Wise. The verse also serves as a warning to the believers not to be complacent. The faithful are not defined by never feeling desire, but by respecting the divine boundaries even when desire pulls them elsewhere. This requires the beautiful patience that the surah commanded the Prophet to have. The patient person restrains himself in the face of temptation, knowing that the lawful outlets are sufficient and that the Hereafter is far better. The connection to the Day of Judgment is again evident: on that Day, the transgressors will face the consequences of their excesses, while those who guarded their chastity will be among those held in honour in the Gardens, as described later. This verse completes the picture of personal purity that is foundational to the faithful character.",
  "32": "The surah continues to paint the portrait of the faithful by highlighting their moral integrity in social dealings: “also those who are true to their trusts and covenants.” The word “amanat” (trusts) covers a vast range of responsibilities—everything that is entrusted to a person by Allah or by other people. This includes the trust of faith itself, the trust of one’s body and faculties, the trust of secrets, property, positions of authority, and contracts. The word “‘ahd” (covenants) refers to solemn agreements, pledges, and promises, whether made with Allah, such as the covenant of faith, or with fellow human beings, such as treaties, business agreements, and marriage contracts. The faithful are those who carefully observe and fulfil these obligations. They do not betray a trust, break a promise, or violate an agreement. This quality is a direct cure for the impatient, selfish nature described earlier. The “halu‘” person is primarily concerned with his own immediate interest; he might betray a trust if it benefits him or break a covenant if it becomes inconvenient. The believer, however, is anchored by the fear of Allah and the belief in the Day of Judgment. He knows that every trust and covenant will be examined on that Day. The Prophet, peace be upon him, was known as Al-Amin, the Trustworthy, even before his prophethood, and this verse reflects the highest standard of character that he exemplified. In the Meccan context, the disbelievers would often break treaties and betray trusts when dealing with the weak. The surah establishes that the community of believers must be completely opposite. Trustworthiness in all matters, big or small, is a non-negotiable quality of those who pray, give zakat, and fear the punishment. It also connects to the earlier theme of wealth: giving the rightful share is part of fulfilling the trust of wealth. The faithful are reliable; their word is their bond. This creates a society of mutual trust and stability, which stands as a living proof of the truth of Islam.",
  "33": "A specific application of trustworthiness is highlighted next: “and who are honest in their testimony.” The Arabic phrase means they stand firm in their testimonies, meaning they give witness truthfully, without distortion, concealment, or bias. Testimony here includes formal legal witness in courts, but it also extends to any statement of truth that affects others’ rights or reputations. The faithful do not let personal feelings, family ties, wealth, or fear of powerful people sway their testimony. They say what is true, even if it is against themselves or their loved ones. This quality is deeply connected to the fear of the punishment of their Lord. A person who fears the Day of Judgment will not lie in testimony, because he knows the ultimate Judge hears every word. The surah earlier described the horrors of that Day, and the faithful keep those horrors in mind when they speak. The disbelievers, on the other hand, often bore false witness to mock the Prophet and oppress the weak. The verse calls the believers to be the exact opposite. This honesty is also a cure for the “halu‘” condition. The impatient, anxious soul might be tempted to twist the truth when it benefits him or to hide facts when they harm his interests. But the believer trains himself to put truth above immediate gain. The placement of this quality after trusts and covenants emphasizes that truthful testimony is one of the most critical trusts. Many injustices in society occur because witnesses fail in their duty. The Quran tells the believers to be standard-bearers of justice, people whose words can be relied upon absolutely. This creates a community where truth is valued and falsehood is exposed. It is an essential part of the beautiful patience and the consistent prayer; a person who stands before Allah five times a day cannot then go and lie with his tongue. The mouth that recites Allah’s words must not be polluted with false testimony.",
  "34": "The list of the faithful’s qualities, which began with prayer, now concludes by returning to prayer: “and who are properly observant of their prayers.” At first glance, this might seem like a repetition of verse twenty-three, which said “consistently performing their prayers.” However, a closer look reveals a different emphasis. The Arabic here uses the word “yuhafizoon,” which means they guard, preserve, and protect their prayers. While consistency referred to the uninterrupted continuity of prayer, this proper observance refers to the quality, the conditions, the timings, and the spiritual presence within the prayer. The faithful do not just perform the prayer habitually; they guard it against negligence, haste, distraction, and anything that would diminish its reward. They maintain its pillars, its recommended acts, and its outward and inward etiquettes. They pray on time, with concentration, fulfilling the requirements of bowing and prostration, and striving for a heart that is present. This bookending of the list with prayer is profoundly beautiful. It shows that prayer is both the beginning and the end, the foundation and the pinnacle of all virtues. All the other qualities—charity, belief in the Day of Judgment, fear, chastity, trustworthiness, honesty—are nourished and protected by the prayer. And the prayer itself needs to be guarded. It is easy to start praying, but the challenge is to maintain its quality over a lifetime. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said, “The first thing the servant will be held accountable for on the Day of Judgment is his prayer. If it is sound, he will succeed; if it is corrupt, he will fail.” This verse reminds the believer that the prayer is a precious treasure that can be lost through carelessness. Guarding it means arranging one’s life around the prayer times, not squeezing prayers into the gaps of a busy schedule. In the context of the surah, this proper observance is what finally cures the impatient, hasty nature. The soul that learns to stand calmly and with full attention before Allah five times a day gradually sheds its restless anxiety and finds its true centre.",
  "35": "After detailing the beautiful qualities of the faithful, the surah announces their reward: “These will be in Gardens, held in honour.” The pronoun “these” points directly to those who embody the characteristics just listed, from prayer to charity to fear of punishment to guarding chastity and trusts. The reward is multifaceted. First, they will be in “Gardens,” the plural indicating expanses of lush, eternal bliss with all that the heart desires. The Garden is the ultimate place of security, the opposite of the restless anxiety of the “halu‘” nature. There will be no distress when touched with evil, and no need to withhold when touched with good, because all good will flow endlessly without fear of loss. Second, they are “held in honour.” The Arabic word “mukramoon” means they are honoured, treated with generosity, and given high status. This is the exact opposite of the humiliation that the disbelievers will face on the Day of Judgment. The mocker who demanded the punishment will be summoned by the raging Flame and have his scalp ripped off, while the faithful believers will be honoured in the presence of Allah. This honour is not just an external gift; it reflects the inner nobility they cultivated through patience, generosity, and moral integrity. They guarded their chastity in private, so Allah will honour them in public. They were honest in their testimony when it was difficult, so Allah will grant them a station of truth. The contrast with earlier verses is powerful. The wicked person wished to ransom himself with his children, spouse, and entire clan. His relationships collapsed. The faithful, however, did not wait for that Day; they gave the rightful share to the poor, maintained their trusts, and prayed constantly. Now they are not bargaining with loved ones in terror; they are together in honour, perhaps reunited with those same loved ones who shared their faith. This verse serves as the climax of the portrait of the faithful, providing a vision of hope that motivates the believer to persevere in these qualities despite the mockery of the disbelievers that will be addressed next.",
  "36": "Having completed the description of the faithful and their reward, the surah turns sharply back to the mockers. The tone becomes confrontational and rhetorical: “So what is the matter with the disbelievers that they rush head-long towards you O Prophet?” The Arabic phrase “fama li allatheena kafaru” expresses astonishment and rebuke. It is as if to say: after all that has been clarified about the Day of Judgment, the punishment, and the qualities of the saved, what excuse do these people have? Why do they behave so strangely? The image is of the disbelievers “rushing” toward the Prophet. In Mecca, whenever the Prophet would recite the Quran or speak about the Hereafter, groups of disbelievers would crowd around him, not to listen sincerely, but to interrupt, to argue, and to mock. The word “muhti‘een” implies that they come with their necks stretched out, eager and hasty, not with the calmness of a seeker of truth but with the agitation of a challenger. This mirrors the “halu‘” nature diagnosed earlier. Their very body language reflects impatience and arrogance. They do not sit and reflect; they rush to attack. The verse exposes their inner state. Their rush is not a sign of genuine interest but a symptom of their disease. The question “what is the matter with them?” is meant to make the listener pause and realize the irrationality of their behaviour. If the Day of Judgment is truly impossible, as they claim, why do they exert so much energy mocking it? Why not simply ignore it? Their frantic rush to disprove the Prophet actually shows that the message is shaking something deep inside them. For the Prophet, this verse brings comfort; Allah is pointing out their absurd behaviour, validating his message and showing him that their mockery is a reflection of their own inner turmoil, not a weakness in the truth.",
  "37": "The description of the disbelievers’ behaviour becomes even more vivid: “from the right and the left, in groups to mock you?” They surround the Prophet from all sides. They come in separate bands, gathering together not for a unified, serious inquiry but to form mocking circles. The phrases “from the right and the left” indicate that they encircle him, leaving no direction free. This could refer to different factions of Quraysh, each approaching from its own angle, all converging on the Prophet with the same hostile intent. They sit in groups, making snide remarks, laughing at the Quran, and challenging his claims. The purpose is explicitly stated: “to mock you.” This historical scene reflects the intense psychological pressure the early Muslims faced. The Prophet was not attacked physically at this stage as much as he was subjected to a relentless campaign of ridicule. The verse exposes the pettiness of their actions. While the surah speaks of a Day fifty thousand years long and a raging Flame, these disbelievers are concerned with forming small groups to sneer. The contrast is deliberate. The faithful are busy with prayer, charity, and guarding their chastity, while the disbelievers are busy forming circles to mock. The verse also carries a subtle warning: just as they now surround the Prophet from right and left in mockery, on the Day of Judgment the punishment will surround them from all sides with no escape. Their mocking groups are a faint and foolish imitation of the encirclement they will later experience. For the believers reading this later, the verse serves as a reminder not to be like those who gather to belittle the truth. Instead, believers should gather to support each other in faith. The mockery is a test of the beautiful patience that was commanded earlier. The Prophet is to endure, and this verse reassures him that Allah sees exactly what they are doing.",
  "38": "The surah now delivers a sharp rhetorical question that exposes the absurdity of the disbelievers’ aspirations: “Does every one of them expect to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?” After detailing the demanding qualities of the faithful who will be in Gardens held in honour, Allah asks whether these mockers think they can attain the same reward without any of those qualities. They reject faith, mock the Messenger, neglect prayer, hoard wealth, and transgress moral boundaries, yet somehow they imagine that if there is a Hereafter, they will be among the honoured? This is the height of self-deception. The question is a powerful piece of logical reasoning. The Garden of Bliss is for those who purified their souls through the beautiful patience and the consistent worship described earlier. The disbelievers have done none of that. By what logic, then, would they enter it? The verse also challenges a common Meccan assumption. The Quraysh sometimes argued that even if resurrection happened, their wealth and status in this world indicated that they were favoured by God and would be favoured in the next as well. This verse demolishes that claim. Worldly prosperity is no sign of Divine approval; it is a test, and their response to the test—mockery and rejection—proves their failure. The word “expect” (yatma‘u) implies a greedy hope, a desire without foundation. It connects back to the description of the human being as “halu‘”: the disbelievers want immediate gratification and also expect eternal bliss with no effort. The verse serves as a wake-up call. It tells them that the Garden of Bliss is not a random lottery; it is the reward for a specific way of life. If they are not willing to walk that path, their expectation is nothing but a delusion. This question also comforts the believers who see the prosperity of the mockers and wonder; Allah assures them that this prosperity does not translate into success in the Hereafter.",
  "39": "The answer to the previous rhetorical question is a decisive “But no!” (Kalla). The disbelievers will not be admitted to the Garden of Bliss with that attitude. Then Allah provides a reason that humbles them: “Indeed, they already know what We created them from.” This refers to their lowly, humble origin. The Quran repeatedly mentions that the human being is created from a drop of despised fluid—from dust and then from a sperm-drop. The disbelievers know this fact perfectly well; it is not a hidden secret. They have seen the process of birth and reproduction. They are aware that their bodies, of which they are so proud, originated from something so insignificant. How, then, can they be so arrogant as to mock the Messenger and feel entitled to the highest paradise? Their knowledge of their humble origin should have led them to humility and gratitude, not to rebellion and self-importance. The verse implies that their intellectual arrogance is inconsistent with their biological reality. If they reflect on their own creation, they will see the power of Allah who brought them from that lowly fluid to a fully formed human being. That same power can certainly resurrect them, and that same Creator deserves their worship, not their mockery. This is a call to self-awareness. The “halu‘” person is so consumed by the present moment—his wealth, his status—that he forgets where he came from and where he is going. The faithful, on the other hand, are constantly reminded by prayer and belief in the Day of Judgment of their origin and their end. The verse also connects to the opening theme: the challenger demanded a punishment, but he should have remembered that he is not a mighty being; he is a creation from a drop of fluid, utterly dependent on the Lord of the pathways of ascent. This knowledge is a proof against them; they have no excuse because the signs are within their own beings.",
  "40": "The surah now reaches a powerful oath that reaffirms the central theme: “So, I do swear by the Lord of all the points of sunrise and sunset that We are truly capable.” The oath is by Allah Himself, described with an attribute that highlights His complete mastery over the cosmos. The “points of sunrise and sunset” refer to the many positions on the horizon where the sun rises and sets throughout the year. As the earth’s tilt and orbit cause the sunrise and sunset points to shift gradually, they pass through numerous distinct locations. The Lord of all these points is the One who governs the entire celestial order with absolute precision. By swearing by this, Allah draws attention to the daily miracle of the sun’s movement, which the disbelievers take for granted. The One who manages this vast, complex system with such flawless regularity is surely “truly capable” of resurrecting the dead and replacing a disobedient people with others better than them. The oath is not made for the sake of the believers, who already believe; it is a rhetorical challenge to the disbelievers who see the Day as impossible. The sky, the sunrise, and the sunset are signs they witness every day. If they deny the resurrection, let them consider the sun’s journey. The phrase “We are truly capable” is emphatic. The capability of Allah is not limited; He who can bring the sun from its many rising points and send it to its many setting points can easily bring forth a new creation. This verse also connects back to the earlier image of the sky becoming like molten brass. The present order, including the orderly sunrise and sunset, will one day be transformed. The Lord of that order can end it and create a new one. The swearing by the Lord of the easts and wests is a profound way to ground the argument in observable reality, making the denial of the resurrection seem all the more unreasonable.",
  "41": "This verse completes the powerful oath that began in the previous verse. After swearing by the Lord of all the points of sunrise and sunset that He is truly capable, Allah now clarifies what that capability entails: “of replacing them with others better than them.” The “them” refers directly to the disbelieving mockers who rush toward the Prophet in groups and arrogantly expect the Garden of Bliss despite their rejection. Allah is fully able to remove them entirely from existence and bring forth a new creation, a people who are better in faith, in obedience, and in character. The word “better” is profound. It shows that the value of a people is not in their lineage, wealth, or worldly power, but in their spiritual quality. The Quraysh prided themselves on being the noblest tribe, the custodians of the Kaaba. They thought they were irreplaceable. This verse shatters that illusion. If they persist in mockery and disbelief, Allah can effortlessly replace them with a community that prays, gives the rightful share of their wealth, believes in the Day of Judgment, fears the punishment, guards their chastity, and is true to their trusts. That community, which at that moment was the small band of persecuted believers, was indeed better in the sight of Allah. History itself fulfilled this warning: the Quraysh leadership that mocked the Prophet was largely destroyed or humbled, and the believing community rose to become the carriers of faith. The phrase “and We cannot be prevented from doing so” adds a final note of absolute sovereignty. No power in the universe can stop Allah from executing His will. The word “masbuq” implies being outrun or overtaken; nothing can outmaneuver the Divine decree. If He decides to replace a people, no one can stand in His way or delay it. This is a stern warning that should terrify those who take Allah’s forbearance for granted. At the same time, it is a source of immense comfort for the oppressed believers. The small, mocked minority is being told that Allah can tip the scales completely. Their patience is not in vain. The verse links back to the command of beautiful patience: the Prophet does not need to retaliate, because Allah’s capability is supreme. The replacement of a corrupt people with a better one is a recurring theme in the Quran, as in the story of Noah and other prophets. This verse, then, is both a promise and a threat, rooted in the undeniable power of the Lord of all easts and wests.",
  "42": "After the strong assertion of Allah’s ability to replace them, the surah delivers a command that carries a terrifying permissiveness: “So let them indulge in falsehood and amuse themselves until they face their Day, which they have been threatened with.” The word “so” connects this directive to the preceding oath of capability. Since Allah is fully able to replace them and since they stubbornly refuse to believe, they are left to their own devices. The command “let them” is not a positive instruction to sin; it is a threat in the form of abandonment. It means: if they choose to remain in their ignorant indulgence and amusement, allow them to do so. Their time is short. They will continue, and the Day of Judgment will arrive suddenly. The phrase “indulge in falsehood” translates the Arabic “yakhudu,” which means to wade deeply into something, to engage in it heedlessly and immersively. They are absorbed in their false talk, their mockery of the Quran, and their denial of the resurrection. They amuse themselves with worldly pleasures, thinking that life is just a game. The verb “amuse themselves” suggests a frivolous, carefree play that distracts from serious reflection. This is the same attitude that the “halu‘” nature produces when unchecked. The verse warns that this period of heedlessness has an endpoint: “until they face their Day.” That Day is not just any day; it is specifically “which they have been threatened with.” The threat has been delivered clearly through the Prophet, backed by vivid descriptions of the molten sky, the mountains like wool, and the raging Flame. They mocked it, but now they are told it is exactly what they will encounter. The tone is chilling. When a parent warns a child repeatedly and the child persists in dangerous play, the parent might finally say, “Fine, do as you wish, and you will see the consequences.” That is the sense here, but multiplied by the eternal weight of the Hereafter. For the believers, this verse is a lesson in detachment. They should not be consumed by anger at the mockers; rather, they should realize that the disbelievers are only being given rope. Their laughter will cease, and they will face what they denied. The verse also implicitly calls the believers to avoid such heedless indulgence. Instead of wading into falsehood and amusement, they are to remain steadfast in prayer and all the noble qualities listed earlier. The contrast between the two groups could not be sharper.",
  "43": "The surah now vividly describes the moment the disbelievers will face their threatened Day: “the Day they will come forth from the graves swiftly, as if racing to an idol for a blessing.” The Day of Judgment begins with the resurrection, when all humanity will burst out of their graves in response to the Divine call. The word “swiftly” (sira‘an) indicates a rapid, almost frantic emergence. There will be no slow, groggy awakening; it will be an urgent, uncontrollable rush. Then comes a startling comparison: “as if racing to an idol for a blessing.” The Arabic word “nusub” refers to the stone altars or idols that the pre-Islamic Arabs used to venerate. They would gather around these idols during their pilgrimage and sacrificial rites, racing toward them and vying for the honour of reaching them first to receive what they believed to be a blessing. The imagery is deeply ironic and layered with meaning. Throughout their lives, these disbelievers hurried toward their false gods, their stone idols, with devotion and zeal. In the Hereafter, they will hurry toward the scene of judgment with the same energy, but this time it will not be to an idol. They will be racing toward their own reckoning. Some commentators also say that “nusub” could refer to a flag or a standard that an army rallies around. They will run as if toward a banner, driven by a force they cannot resist. The verse exposes the habit of misplaced devotion. The energy they spent on false worship in this world will be mirrored in the helpless urgency on that Day, but now it brings no benefit. They ran to stones that could neither harm nor help them, and now they run to face the punishment they denied. Another layer of the comparison is the sheer speed and single-mindedness. In the pre-Islamic pilgrimage, tribes would race to the idol to gain prestige. Here, all of humanity races toward the gathering place with a similar haste, but with a vastly different destination. The verse thus mocks their earlier behaviour while warning of the inevitable. For the believer, it is a reminder to direct one’s urgency and devotion toward the true goal: the pleasure of Allah, not the false idols of this world. It also reinforces that the Day of Judgment is not a calm, ordered assembly; it is a terrifying, chaotic emergence that strips away all human pride.",
  "44": "This final verse of the surah zooms in on the physical and emotional state of the disbelievers on that Day: “with eyes downcast, utterly covered with disgrace.” The Arabic word “khashi‘ah” describes eyes that are humiliated, lowered, and staring downward in abject shame. In this world, the disbelievers’ eyes were full of arrogance and mockery; they would glance at the Prophet and the believers with contempt and pride. On that Day, the direction of their gaze will be reversed. They will not dare to look up. Their eyes will be fixed on the ground, weighed down by the horror of their situation. The phrase “utterly covered with disgrace” (tarhaquhum dhillah) paints a picture of disgrace clinging to them like a garment, enveloping them completely. The word “dhillah” is intense humiliation, the complete opposite of the honour (ikram) that the faithful will enjoy in the Gardens, as mentioned in verse thirty-five. This disgrace is not just an external punishment; it is an internal state of worthlessness and regret that overwhelms them. They are not just physically punished; their very souls are crushed with shame. The contrast with the believers is stark. The believers were those who guarded their chastity, were honest in their testimony, and feared their Lord’s punishment. They walked on earth with modesty and dignity. Now they are held in honour. The disbelievers, who strutted arrogantly and mocked the truth, are now covered in disgrace. The final sentence, “That is the Day they have always been warned of,” brings the entire surah full circle. From the very first verse, where a challenger demanded a punishment bound to come, the surah has been describing this Day and warning of it. Every image—the molten sky, the severed family ties, the raging Flame ripping scalps—was a warning. Now the surah closes by reminding them that all those warnings were true. The warning came through the Prophet and through the Quran, but they treated it as a joke. Now they see it with their own downcast eyes, and it is too late. This conclusion serves as both a final, sobering threat to the disbelievers and a profound reassurance to the believers. The believers endured mockery and hardship while clinging to beautiful patience, and they are now vindicated. The surah that began with a mocker’s challenge ends with the absolute fulfilment of the warning he mocked. It is a masterful structure, leaving the listener with an overwhelming sense of the reality and certainty of the Hereafter."
};
